WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:03.159
Truly great film trilogies are few and far
between.

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:06.140
Sure, there are standouts like Star Wars and
Lord of the Rings.

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:10.160
But besides those, an unblemished trilogy
is a shockingly rare thing.

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:13.580
Plenty of franchises go straight downhill
after the opening chapter.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:17.580
What's even worse, though, is a trilogy that's
held back from perfection by a single entry.

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:21.900
It's maddening for fans of the story being
told, and more often than not, it denies fans

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:24.360
the story structure or resolution they desire.

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:28.500
With all that in mind, here's a look at some
memorable trilogies ruined by one movie.

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:30.100
Spider-Man

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:35.580
It's hard to overstate the massive success
of Sam Raimi's first two Spider-Man films.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:39.040
They were crucial in creating the modern superhero
boom as we know it.

00:00:39.140 --> 00:00:43.480
Spider-Man and Spider-Man 2 showed modern
audiences that the genre could still work

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:45.860
in the wake of '90s bombs like Batman &amp; Robin.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:48.460
Watching them today, it's easy to understand
why.

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:52.660
They're clear labors of love, and with the
second chapter ending on a cliffhanger for

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:56.720
the ages, it seemed that the third installment
was primed for a killer finale.

00:00:57.020 --> 00:01:00.880
Unfortunately, Raimi butted heads with the
studio during production of Spider-Man 3.

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:05.440
He was ultimately forced to include popular
comics villain Venom, making for a bloated

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:08.920
and convoluted story that lacks the heart
of the previous two installments.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:13.420
There's simply too much going on, and the
campier moments are tonally jarring against

00:01:13.420 --> 00:01:14.420
the more serious elements.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:28.079
While a fourth installment was considered
and planned, Sony ultimately decided to abandon

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:31.260
Raimi's Spidey for a rebooted model under
new director Marc Webb.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:33.320
Blade

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:37.819
They may not get the respect that the X-Men
and Spidey movies do, but the first two Blade

00:01:37.820 --> 00:01:41.220
films are just as integral to the modern superhero
movie era.

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:45.859
While directors Stephen Norrington and Guillermo
del Toro deserve a great deal of credit for

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:50.340
making the films work as well as they do,
the success really comes down to Wesley Snipes.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:54.660
He embodies the calm, collected cool of the
character with a performance that feels effortless.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:57.980
But it all went wrong in the third installment,
Blade: Trinity.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:02.800
Set drama and a studio feud with Snipes saw
writer David S. Goyer step in as a last-minute

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:04.030
replacement director.

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:08.480
The result is a deeply disappointing conclusion
to a trilogy, and a character, that deserves

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:08.980
better.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:15.660
"You gotta be kidding me"

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:21.920
Aside from a quickly canceled TV show, we
haven't seen Blade onscreen since.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:23.900
Iron Man

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:28.560
There are few superhero origin films as perfect
as 2008's Iron Man.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:32.819
It's the film that gave us Robert Downey Jr.'s
Tony Stark, one of the all-time great pieces

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:33.820
of movie casting.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:36.600
As Stark, Downey could read the phonebook
and make it fun.

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:41.000
Still, Iron Man 2 is something of a mess,
and the only thing stopping the Iron Man trilogy

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.800
from being one of the best of all time.

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:46.420
It's a film clearly burdened by expectations
and responsibility.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:50.500
It has to introduce Black Widow to the MCU
and expand other side characters.

00:02:50.730 --> 00:02:55.140
On top of that is an ambitious but utterly
nonsensical plot involving the invention of

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:55.940
a new element.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:58.180
"This is the key to the future.

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:03.880
I'm limited by the technology of my time.

00:03:04.620 --> 00:03:06.520
But one day you'll figure this out."

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:11.150
Iron Man 2 didn't tank the MCU by any means,
but it's early evidence that these films are

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:12.120
far from infallible.

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:14.060
X-Men

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:18.450
It's pretty standard for blockbuster films
to announce their release date years in advance

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:21.340
these days, long before a creative team has
been assembled.

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:24.340
It's crucial to remember, though, that that
wasn't always the case.

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:29.099
After the first two films in the X-Men franchise
were met with critical acclaim and great financial

00:03:29.099 --> 00:03:33.610
success, the trilogy's conclusion, The Last
Stand, was slated for release before the script

00:03:33.610 --> 00:03:34.540
had been finalized.

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:36.240
This turned out to be its downfall.

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:40.460
After Brian Singer and Matthew Vaughn both
dropped out of directing, Fox finally settled

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:43.640
on Brett Ratner, who at that point barely
had a year to make the film.

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:48.560
Multiple cast members now had scheduling conflicts
as well, leading to some strange character

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:50.180
rewrites to account for their absences.

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:55.020
That the film came out at all is a small miracle
and certainly commendable in a certain light.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.400
However, the end product is still pretty lousy.

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:00.760
"Don't let it control you."

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:13.859
It was such a disaster that it effectively
tanked this cinematic incarnation of the X-Men

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:18.300
until they reappeared for a brief swan song
in 2014's Days of Future Past.

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:20.460
Men in Black

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:25.080
Will Smith will always be a huge star, but
he was a special kind of huge star in the

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:25.580
'90s.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:28.620
Any project he led was more or less guaranteed
to succeed.

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:33.540
Among the biggest of his hits is 1997's stylish
sci-fi action comedy Men in Black.

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:38.080
He starred as loudmouthed Agent J, a brilliant
foil to his straight and narrow partner, Agent

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:40.360
K, played to perfection by Tommy Lee Jones.

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:45.660
Fifteen years later, Men in Black III arrived,
wrapping up the story of Agents J and K perfectly,

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:49.610
due in no small part to a killer performance
by Josh Brolin as a younger K.

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:53.180
"We'll take it from here"

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:54.740
"K?

00:04:57.100 --> 00:04:57.600
K?!

00:04:58.000 --> 00:04:58.500
K!

00:04:58.500 --> 00:04:59.060
AAHH!"

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:02.440
Why did it take 15 years for this hit trilogy
to conclude?

00:05:02.780 --> 00:05:06.220
One answer, unfortunately, is that the second
film is downright lousy.

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:10.470
Men in Black II feels like a recycled version
of its predecessor with none of the charm

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:11.100
or surprise.

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:15.300
It may have been a box office hit, but it
didn't exactly leave audiences hungry for

00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:15.820
more.

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:17.580
"Tell me something we don't know."

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:19.500
"Ya, tell me something we don't know.

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:21.360
I'm so sorry."

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:22.260
"God bless you."

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:23.560
"Thank you."

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:27.840
There's no telling the heights the franchise
could have reached had the second film not

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:29.060
dropped the ball so hard.

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:31.020
Thor

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:35.760
The first installment in the Thor trilogy
may not be the best the MCU has to offer,

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:39.860
but it's a solid origin story with Shakespearean
flair thanks to director Kenneth Branagh.

00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:44.340
It almost feels quaint: a quiet little character
study about an arrogant boy who wants to be

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:45.140
king but isn't ready.

00:05:45.460 --> 00:05:49.740
Thor: Ragnarok, on the other hand, is a nutty
cosmic romp full of laughs and unparalleled

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:51.410
art direction and visual design.

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:56.220
Thor: The Dark World is the black sheep of
the trilogy, not so much bad as it is dull.

00:05:56.340 --> 00:05:59.080
It fails to build on the sense of wonder instilled
in the first film.

00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:02.960
It feels bland and by the numbers, and it
often looks like someone scribbled over the

00:06:02.980 --> 00:06:05.340
first film's bright color palette with a grey
crayon.

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:07.510
Frankly, it may be the worst movie in the
MCU.

00:06:07.510 --> 00:06:09.300
"What the hell was that?!

00:06:11.080 --> 00:06:12.200
And why are you smiling?"

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:16.580
"There's nothing more reassuring than realizing
that the world is crazier than you are."

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:20.190
The Dark World exhausts the old version of
what a Thor movie should be, to the point

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:22.860
that Ragnarok had no choice but to try something
new.

00:06:23.140 --> 00:06:27.100
Ultimately it's a good thing that Ragnarok
was able to happen, even if it meant enduring

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:28.680
a pretty bland Thor movie first.

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:32.680
Still, it's hard not to wish the Thor trilogy
had maintained more consistency.

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:35.020
The Ocean Trilogy

00:06:35.540 --> 00:06:39.960
When the right performers, script, and director
align, a movie can feel effortlessly fun.

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:43.520
Steven Soderbergh's Vegas heist film Ocean's
11 is one of those movies.

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:48.280
Featuring a killer cast that includes George
Clooney, Brad Pitt, Matt Damon, Julia Roberts,

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:53.240
and Andy Garcia, Ocean's 11 is so entertaining
that it's almost impossible to turn away from.

00:06:53.650 --> 00:06:58.460
Its second sequel, Ocean's 13, is similarly
rewatchable, aided greatly by the inclusion

00:06:58.460 --> 00:06:59.860
of Al Pacino as the villain.

00:07:00.040 --> 00:07:03.160
Ocean's 12, however, very nearly spoils the
stew.

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:07.310
You can't help but get the feeling when watching
it that Soderbergh and company wanted an excuse

00:07:07.310 --> 00:07:11.120
to hang out in Europe and went on to tailor
the film to suit their vacation needs.

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:15.320
Everyone seems to be having a good time, but
the spark present in the first and third films

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:15.880
is gone.

00:07:15.980 --> 00:07:21.120
Throw in a genuinely bizarre subplot involving
Julia Roberts' character masquerading as Julia

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:25.281
Roberts, and you've got a shockingly lousy
film as the direct follow-up to one of the

00:07:25.281 --> 00:07:27.060
most entertaining movies of the century.

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:28.800
The Matrix

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:33.220
The Matrix changed the game upon its release
in 1999.

00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:34.820
Even today, it feels like a revelation.

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:39.720
It pushes genre boundaries, melding high-minded
science fiction with breathtaking action shot

00:07:39.720 --> 00:07:41.400
in the innovative "bullet time" style.

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:46.430
It gives us a cast of memorable characters
and injects themes of existentialism and Buddhism

00:07:46.430 --> 00:07:48.800
into what could have been a cut-and-dry sci-fi
adventure.

00:07:48.980 --> 00:07:53.340
Its first sequel, The Matrix Reloaded, doesn't
quite reach the heights of its predecessor,

00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:54.420
but then again, what could?

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:58.400
It's still a fun, interesting action romp
that expands on the themes introduced in the

00:07:58.400 --> 00:07:58.940
first film.

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:03.360
It all comes crashing down with The Matrix
Revolutions, a bloated mess that's too long

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:05.660
and devoid of anything meaningful or important.

00:08:05.910 --> 00:08:10.120
The transgressive themes of the franchise
are cast aside for more special effects, eventually

00:08:10.130 --> 00:08:13.699
oversaturating the film, and very nearly the
trilogy as a whole.

00:08:13.699 --> 00:08:18.380
It fails to deliver a satisfying ending to
a story its predecessors got audiences deeply

00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:22.470
invested in, and the fact that Neo's journey
never got the resolution it deserved is one

00:08:22.470 --> 00:08:25.260
of the true missed opportunities in recent
film history.

00:08:26.660 --> 00:08:27.620
"Is it over?"

